      "id": "verbosity_explain_water",
      "category": "verbosity_fingerprint",
      "check_type": "length_range",
      "passed": false,
      "errored": false,
      "error": null,
      "latency_ms": 2803.5,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 655,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound made up of two elements: **hydrogen and oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**, meaning every single molecule of water consists of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom. \n\nHowever, water is much more than a simple chemical formula. It is the defining substance of our planet and the foundation of all known life. Here is a breakdown of what water is, why it’s unique, and why it matters:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Physical and Chemical Properties\nWater is unique because of how it behaves under normal conditions on Earth:\n* **The Three States:** Water is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three physical states: **liquid** (water), **solid** (ice), and **gas** (steam or vapor).\n* **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Because of its chemical structure, water is exceptionally good at dissolving other substances (like salt, sugar, and minerals). This ability is what allows it to transport nutrients and chemicals in living things and in the environment.\n* **Density Anomaly:** Most substances shrink and become denser when they freeze, but water actually *expands* and becomes less dense. This is why ice floats on liquid water, a crucial feature that prevents lakes and oceans from freezing solid from the bottom up, allowing marine life to survive the winter.\n* **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb and release large amounts of heat without a drastic change in temperature. This helps regulate the climate of the entire planet and keeps body temperatures stable in living organisms.\n\n### 2. Water on Earth\nWater covers about **71% of the Earth's surface**, primarily in oceans and seas (which hold about 96.5% of all Earth's water). \n* Only about **2.5% of Earth's water is freshwater**, and most of that is locked away in glaciers, ice caps, or buried deep underground. \n* Less than 1% of Earth's water is easily accessible freshwater in lakes, rivers, and the atmosphere for human use.\n* Water is constantly moving through the **water cycle** (evaporation, condensation, precipitation, and collection), meaning the water you drink today is the same water that dinosaurs drank millions of years ago.\n\n### 3. Importance to Life\nWater is often called the \"elixir of life.\" No known form of life can survive without it.\n* **Biological Roles:** In the human body, water makes up about 60% of adult weight. It acts as a lubricant for joints, regulates body temperature, and flushes out waste. Inside cells, it provides the medium where all the chemical reactions of life take place.\n* **Evolution:** Life on Earth began in the oceans, and the internal fluid of most living creatures (like blood and cell fluid) is largely salt water, echoing our ancient origins.\n\n### Summary\nIn short, water is a simple molecule with extraordinary properties. It is a dynamic, life-giving substance that shapes our planet’s geology, drives our weather, and makes the existence of biological creatures possible."
